  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 445 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nHeat impacts:\nAnother warm/hot afternoon across the area...and also quite\nsteamy for most of south central Nebraska, where dew points in\nthe upper 60s to mid 70s are common. Air temperatures have\nsurpassed the century mark for many areas along and S of the\nstate line, but this is also where drier air resides. Thus, heat\nindices here are not all that different than the thermometer\nreadings. Expanded the Heat Advisory earlier today to include\nthe rest of northern KS and sure enough, both Phillipsburg and\nSmith Center are hovering near 105F heat index. Will be\ninteresting to see how the heat unfolds next couple of days as\nthere will be several competing factors. A cold front (currently\nalong the Hwy 20 corridor) will gradually sink southward and\nusher in at least modestly cooler air for most of our Neb\nzones...and there could also be several bouts of mid to high\nclouds and perhaps even a few elevated showers. However, areas S\nof the boundary will remain very warm, and likely as humid or\neven more humid than today given propensity for moisture to pool\nalong/just ahead of such boundaries. Also, these areas will\nremain quite warm tonight - only briefly dipping into the upper\n70s - and have an already elevated launching pad for\ntemperatures on Wednesday. Therefore, opted to issued another\nHeat Advisory for Osborne, Mitchell, and Jewell Counties where\nheat indices around 105F are again most likely. It's possible\nthe front may not move quite as far S as currently expected, in\nwhich case the advisory may very well need to be expanded\nnorthward. We'll see how the front trends overnight.\n\nTemperature uncertainties only increase for Thursday, with\nlatest NBM guidance indicating the potential for a 25+ degree\nspread in highs from N to S. In fact, the current forecast for\nThursday would place our far S tier of KS counties once again\naround the 105F heat index criteria for another Heat Advisory.\nConsiderably higher chances for showers and thunderstorms Wed\nnight (discussed further below) cast even more uncertainty on \nwhere the effective front/composite outflow resides by peak \nheating Thursday. Just know the far S may need to wait one more\nday (maybe even two??) to get in on some relief from the heat.\nThe latest forecast is void of any 90s by Sunday-Monday, and\nareas along/N of I-80 may not get out of the 70s. Pattern may\nturn warmer again towards the middle of next week.\n\nRain/Storm Chances:\nScattered showers and storms are developing over W Neb. late\nthis afternoon, and this activity will attempt to move into at\nleast far W zones this evening. However, similar to past few\nnights, the expectation is that steady weakening will occur\nafter sunset. Thus, may not see much more than sprinkles E of\nHwy 183. Another area of storms for us to watch will be what's\ncurrently developing over the Black Hills. Most CAM guidance\nsuggests the brunt of this will remain to our N (thus only being\ngrazed by the Day 1 Marginal Risk)...but this batch is expected\nto be more organized...and sometimes these complexes can turn a\nlittle more southerly than models indicate.\n\nCan't rule out some elevated showers/weak storms over portions\nof south central Nebraska during the day Wednesday, but\nsignificantly higher chances for rain arrive on Wednesday night.\nAppears this activity will focus the most over areas mainly\nW/NW/N of the Tri-Cities, but the entire forecast area will at\nleast stand a chance of seeing something. There is a Slight Risk\n(level 2 of 5) for severe weather for the aforementioned most-\nfavorable corridor, with the main risk being damaging winds and\nlarge hail. The combination of strong instability and \nseasonably strong deep layer shear argues for at least SOME \nrisk for a more organized upscale growth/MCS and damaging wind \nthreat to evolve out of the initial development over W NE/SD Wed\neve- night. However, there remains some uncertainty on the \noverall strength and placement of the low level jet, which may\nbe displaced further E. The general pattern, conceptually, is\none that favors MCSs rolling off the High Plains, so this is a\ntrend we'll need to monitor closely, esp. for areas N of I-80.\n\nFrequent, essentially daily, rain/storm chances continue\nThursday eve/night and into the weekend. Finer details are\nmurky, at best...but would expect efficient rain makers given \ncoherent connection to monsoonal moisture that's pulled \nnorthward on the backside of the high pressure over the Deep \nSouth. Also, proximity to seasonably strong mid/upper flow\nargues for non-zero severe risk, as well...esp. as it pertains\nto possibly one or two more organized MCSs in the evening and\novernight hours. Upper ridging may begin to reassert itself and\nlead to lower rain chances next week.",
